@H@T0This help file contains the most often used offsets into OS-9 memory modules.The module header is the same for all type of modules:  Offset  Size  Name       Description  ------  ----  ---------  --------------------------------------------------   0/$00  word  M$ID       Sync Bytes ($4AFC = M$ID12)   2/$02  word  M$SysRev   Module format Revision ID   4/$04  long  M$Size     Module size in bytes   8/$08  long  M$Owner    Owner ID  12/$0C  long  M$Name     Offset to module name string  16/$10  word  M$Accs     Access permissions  18/$12  byte  M$Type     Module Type: 1=Prgrm, 2=Sbrtn, 3=Multi, 4=Data                                        5=CSDData, 11=TrapLib, 12=Systm                                        13=Flmgr, 14=Drivr, 15=Devic  19/$13  byte  M$Lang     Language: 0=Objct,2=ICode,3=PCode, >3=future.  20/$14  byte  M$Attr     Attributes: ReEntBit=7, GhostBit=6, SupStBit=5  21/$15  byte  M$Revs     Revision level  22/$16  word  M$Edit     Edition number  24/$18  long  M$Usage    Offset to usage string  28/$1C  long  M$Symbol   Offset to symbol table (not in use)  32/$20  14    -           reserved (7 words)  46/$2E  word  M$Parity   Header parity check  48/$30                   module type dependent (see subtopics)@D1Executables : 2Init_Module : 3Descriptors : 4 , 5@T2Executable modules can be Traphandlers, Programs, Device Drivers, FileManagers and System Modules.For all executable modules:  Offset  Size  Name       Description  ------  ----  ---------  --------------------------------------------------  48/$30  long  M$Exec     Execution offset  52/$34  long  M$Excpt    Default User Trap Execution Entry PointFor device drivers, programs and traphandlers:  Offset  Size  Name       Description  ------  ----  ---------  --------------------------------------------------  56/$38  long  M$Mem      Memory Size (data space)For programs and traphandlers:  Offset  Size  Name       Description  ------  ----  ---------  --------------------------------------------------  60/$3C  long  M$Stack    Stack size (stack data space)  64/$40  long  M$IData    Offset to initialisation data (in the module)  68/$44  long  M$IRefs    Offset to reference initialisation dataFor traphandlers only:  72/$48  long  M$Init     Offset to Traphandler initialisation routine  76/$4C  long  M$Term     Offset to Traphandler termination routine@T3The init module contains system startup parameters.  Offset  Size  Name       Description  ------  ----  ---------  --------------------------------------------------  48/$30  long  -          reserved  52/$34  word  M$Pollsz   Number of IRQ polling table entries  54/$36  word  M$DevCnt   Number of entries in the device table  56/$38  word  M$Procs    Initial process table size (in # of processes)  58/$3A  word  M$Paths    Initial path table size (in # of paths)  60/$3C  word  M$Sparam   Offset to parameter string for startup module  62/$3E  word  M$SysGo    Offset to name of startup module (usually: "sysgo")  64/$40  word  M$SysDev   Offset to path of system device (usually a disk)  66/$42  word  M$Consol   Offset to initial standard I/O path (often "/term")  68/$44  word  M$Extens   Offset to name of customisation module  70/$46  word  M$Clock    Offset to name of clock module  72/$48  word  M$Slice    Number of ticks per time slice  74/$4A  6     -          reserved (3 words)  80/$50  word  M$Instal   Offset to installation name string  82/$52  long  M$CPUTyp   CPU Type: 68000,68010,68020,68030 (as a number !)  86/$56  long  M$OS9Lvl   Byte0=Level, Byte1.Byte2=Version, Byte3=Edition  90/$5A  word  M$OS9Rev   Offset to revision name string  92/$5C  word  M$SysPri   Initial system priority  94/$5E  word  M$MinPty   Initial system minimum executable priority  96/$60  word  M$MaxAge   Initial system maximal natural age  98/$62  4     -          reserved (2 words) 102/$66  word  M$Events   Initial event table size (in # of events) 104/$68  byte  M$Compat   Revision compatibility byte: Bit0:1=save regs on IRQ                           Bit1:1=ignore ghost bit, Bit2:1=kernel doesn't STOP                           Bit3:1=no cache burst (68030), Bit4:1=memory fill on                           allocate/deallocate, Bit5:1=do not start clock. 105/$69  byte  -          reserved 106/$6A  word  M$MemList  Offset to colored memory list (0 if none) 108/$6C  word  M$IRQStk   Size of IRQ stack (in long words) 110/$6E  word  M$ColdTrys # of retries to CHD to system device at coldstart 112/$70  20    -          reserved here the strings (+ ev. the colored memory list) referenced above will follow.@T4All device descriptors have the following module offsets in common:  Offset  Size  Name       Description  ------  ----  ---------  --------------------------------------------------  48/$30  long  M$Port     absolute port address of the device  52/$34  byte  M$Vector   Vector number for IRQ  53/$35  byte  M$IRQLvl   IRQ interrupt level  54/$36  byte  M$Prior    IRQ polling priority  55/$37  byte  M$Mode     Device mode capabilities (Bits: dsi--ewr)  56/$38  word  M$FMgr     Offset to file manager name  58/$3A  word  M$PDev     Offset to device driver name  60/$3C  word  M$DevCon   Offset to device dependent configuration data  62/$3E  8     -          reserved (4 words)  70/$46  word  M$Opt      number of option bytes  72/$48  byte  M$DTyp     device type  (0=SCF, 1=RBF, 2=PIPE, 3=SBF, 4=NFM)  the following fields are dependent on the file manager (see subtopics).@D5 W=19SCF_Descriptor : 6RBF_Descriptor : 7SBF_Descriptor : 8@T6The following fields are specific for SCF device descriptors.(*) The names (PD_xxx) refer to offsets into the path descriptor and therefore    are not module offsets. Use the following expression to obtain real device    descriptor offsets: PD_xxx-PD_Opt+M$Opt. Please note also that the numbers    in the "Offset column" *are* module offsets, not path descriptor offsets!  Offset  Size  Name (*)   Description  ------  ----  ---------  --------------------------------------------------  72/$48  byte  PD_DTP     Device type (=same offset as M$DTyp), value=0 (SCF)  73/$49  byte  PD_UPC     if<>0: uppercase lock is on.  74/$4A  byte  PD_BSO     if=0: SCF echoes PD_BSE for backspace (PD_BSP)                           if<>0: SCF echoes PD_BSE, space, PD_BSE  75/$4B  byte  PD_DLO     if=0: SCF erases line using repeated backspace                           if<>0: SCF echoes CR/LF when the line is deleted  76/$4C  byte  PD_EKO     if<>0: input characters will be echoed  77/$4D  byte  PD_ALF     if<>0: automatic linefeed sent after CR  78/$4E  byte  PD_NUL     # of null ($00) chars to be sent after CR/LF  79/$4F  byte  PD_PAU     if<>0: auto page pause enabled (after PD_PAG lines)  80/$50  byte  PD_PAG     page length in lines (used for auto page pause)  81/$51  byte  PD_BSP     Backspace input character (usually ^H)  82/$52  byte  PD_DEL     Delete line input character (usually ^X)  83/$53  byte  PD_EOR     End of record input character (alomst always CR=^M)  84/$54  byte  PD_EOF     End of file input character (usually ESC=^[ or ^Z)  85/$55  byte  PD_RPR     Reprint line input character (usually ^R)  86/$56  byte  PD_DUP     Duplicate from previous line input char (usually ^A)  87/$57  byte  PD_PSC     Pause character (usually ^W)  88/$58  byte  PD_INT     Keyboard interrupt (S$Intrpt=3, usually ^C)  89/$59  byte  PD_QUT     Keyboard abort character (S$Abort=2, usually ^E)  90/$5A  byte  PD_BSE     Backspace echo character (often ^H)  91/$5B  byte  PD_OVF     Line overflow signal character (usually BEL=^G)  92/$5C  byte  PD_PAR     Serial Params: Bits1&0: 00=no 01=odd, 11=even parity                                          Bits3&2: 00=8, 01=7, 02=6, 03=5 bits                                          Bits5&4: 00=1, 01=1.5, 10=2 stop bits                                          Bits7&6: reserved  93/$5D  byte  PD_BAU     Baudrate: 0=50, 1=75, 2=110, 3=134.5, 4=150, 5=300,                                     6=600, 7=1200, 8=1800, 9=2000, A=2400,                                     B=3600, C=4800, D=7200, E=9600, F=19200,                                     FF=external  94/$5E  word  PD_D2P     Output device name string offset (usually=M$Name)  96/$60  byte  PD_XON     X-on character (0=none, usually DC1=^Q)  97/$61  byte  PD_XOFF    X-off character (0=none, usually DC3=^S)  98/$62  byte  PD_Tab     Tab character (usually ^I)  99/$63  byte  PD_Tabs    Tab field size (usually 4)@T7The following fields are specific for RBF device descriptors.(*) The names (PD_xxx) refer to offsets into the path descriptor and therefore    are not module offsets. Use the following expression to obtain real device    descriptor offsets: PD_xxx-PD_Opt+M$Opt. Please note also that the numbers    in the "Offset column" *are* module offsets, not path descriptor offsets!  Offset  Size  Name (*)   Description  ------  ----  ---------  --------------------------------------------------  72/$48  byte  PD_DTP     Device type (=same offset as M$DTyp), value=1 (RBF)  73/$49  byte  PD_DRV     Drive number (0..n-1, where n= max # of drives)  74/$4A  byte  PD_STP     Step rate (0=fast..3=slow, see manual)  75/$4B  byte  PD_TYP     Disk type: Bit0: 0=5"floppy, 1=8"floppy                                      Bit5: 0=track0 SD, 1=track0 DD                                      Bit7: 0=floppy disk, 1=hard disk  76/$4C  byte  PD_DNS     Density:   Bit0: 0=SD, 1=DD                                      Bit1: 0=40 Track, 1=80 Track (48/96TpI)                                      Bit2: reserved for quad density  77/$4D  byte  -          reserved  78/$4E  word  PD_CYL     Number of cylinders  80/$50  byte  PD_SID     Number of heads (harddisks) or sides (floppies)  81/$51  byte  PD_VFY     0=verify disk writes, 1=don't verify  82/$52  word  PD_SCT     Number of sectors per track (default)  84/$54  word  PD_TOS     Number of sectors on track 0 (default)  86/$56  word  PD_SAS     Segment allocation size  88/$58  byte  PD_ILV     Sector interleave factor (1 = no interleaving)  89/$59  byte  PD_TFM     DMA transfer mode (implementation specific)  90/$5A  byte  PD_TOffs   Track base offset  91/$5B  byte  PD_SOffs   Sector base offset  92/$5C  word  PD_SSize   Sector size in bytes (currently fixed on 256)  94/$5E  word  PD_Cntl    Control word: Bit0: 1=formatting disabled                                         Bit1: 1=multisector I/O possible                                         Bit3: 1=autosizing media                                         Bit4: 1=can format single track  96/$60  byte  PD_Trys    Number of retries: 0=default, 1=none, 2..255=#trys  97/$61  byte  PD_LUN     Logical Unit number of SCSI Drive  98/$62  word  PD_WPC     Cylinder to begin write precomensation 100/$64  word  PD_RWR     Cylinder to begin reduced write current 102/$66  word  PD_PARK    Cylinder to park disk head 104/$68  long  PD_LSNOffs Logical sector offset (for partitioned SCSI drives) 108/$6C  word  PD_TotCyls Total number of cylinders on partitioned SCSI drive 110/$6E  byte  PD_CtrlrID SCSI controller ID (bitmask/number, impl.dependent) 112/$70  long  PD_ScsiOpt SCSI option flags: Bit0: 1=SCSI parity enabled                                              Bit1: 1=ATN support enabled                                              Bit2: 1=synchronous transfer                                              Bit3: 1=target mode supportedhere device specific data may follow if M$DevCon is<>0. This often includesadditional parameters for SCSI controller setup etc. DevCon data is highlyhardware and implementation specific.@T8The following fields are specific for SBF device descriptors.(*) The names (PD_xxx) refer to offsets into the path descriptor and therefore    are not module offsets. Use the following expression to obtain real device    descriptor offsets: PD_xxx-PD_Opt+M$Opt. Please note also that the numbers    in the "Offset column" *are* module offsets, not path descriptor offsets!  Offset  Size  Name (*)   Description  ------  ----  ---------  --------------------------------------------------  72/$48  byte  PD_DTP     Device type (=same offset as M$DTyp), value=3 (SBF)  73/$49  byte  PD_TDrv    Tape Drive number (0..n-1, where n= max # of drives)  74/$4A  byte  PD_SBF     reserved  75/$4B  byte  PD_NumBlk  Maximum number of blocks to allocate  76/$4C  long  PD_BlkSiz  Size of blocks allocated  80/$50  word  PD_Prior   Driver process priority  82/$52  byte  PD_Flags   Drive capability flags: Bit0: 1=rewind on close                                                   Bit1: 1=offline on close                                                   Bit2: 1=erase to eot on clos  83/$53  byte  -          reserved for driver  84/$54  word  PD_DMAMode DMA mode. Hardware and implementation specific  86/$56  byte  PD_ScsiID  SCSI controller ID (bitmask/number, impl.dependent)  87/$57  byte  PD_ScsiLUN Logical Unit number of SCSI Tape Drive  88/$58  long  ??         SCSI option flags: Bit0: 1=SCSI parity enabled                                              Bit1: 1=ATN support enabled                                              Bit2: 1=synchronous transfer                                              Bit3: 1=target mode supported